/* Lanternweald pass 14 B: six interlocking lived-in chapters with distinct voices. */
(function(){'use strict';const data=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!data||!Array.isArray(data.series))return;const revisions={"love-and-duty":{"love-and-duty-46":{"number":46,"title":"Dinner for People Who Disagree","content":"Katara came home carrying a bag of dried fish and three new reasons to argue with the council.\n\nI met her at the Riverstone Door.\n\nFor one second I forgot everything except the fact that she was standing there. Her hair was windblown, her cheeks red from the journey, and the strap of her travel bag had left a mark across her shoulder.\n\n\"You're home,\" I said.\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nI hugged her before I remembered to ask.\n\nShe laughed against my neck and held me tight.\n\n\"Missed you,\" she whispered.\n\n\"Missed you too.\"\n\nWhen we stepped apart, she looked over my shoulder.\n\n\"Where are the others?\"\n\n\"Aang is trying to convince Toph that a dinner menu can be planned without threats.\"\n\n\"That sounds promising.\"\n\n\"It is not.\"\n\nKatara grinned.\n\nThe Southern clinic had enough trained staff to manage the next phase without her. She'd helped set up a treatment room, trained two local healers in the new record system, and arranged for supplies that were actually available rather than inventing a list of things nobody could deliver. The work would continue without her; that mattered.\n\nAt the house, Toph heard us before we reached the steps.\n\n\"Well, look who remembered the way home.\"\n\nKatara set down her bag.\n\n\"Missed you too.\"\n\nToph crossed the room in three quick strides and hugged her, muttering something about sea air and terrible timing.\n\nAang came from the kitchen with flour on his sleeve.\n\nHe stopped.\n\nFor once, he had no words at all.\n\nKatara looked at him.\n\n\"Hello, Aang.\"\n\nHe crossed the room and hugged her so carefully that she laughed.\n\n\"I'm not made of glass.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You're holding me like a teacup.\"\n\n\"Then let me put you down before I spill.\"\n\nWe all laughed.\n\nI wished the evening could have stayed there.\n\nInstead, Iroh arrived for dinner, and the first argument began over the place settings.\n\nToph wanted to sit nearest the open window because it gave her the best sense of the street below. Katara wanted the window closed because the evening was cold. Aang wanted the table moved so no one had to sit with their back to the door. I had planned six dishes and only counted five bowls.\n\nIroh examined the scene with a smile.\n\n\"May I offer a suggestion?\"\n\n\"No,\" Toph said.\n\n\"Excellent. I shall keep it to myself.\"\n\nThat made her laugh.\n\nWe moved the table. We kept the window open a finger's width. Aang fetched another bowl. Iroh poured tea while Katara unpacked the dried fish she'd brought from home, which Toph promptly declared proof that Katara had missed us in a practical and delicious way.\n\nDinner began.\n\nThen the council came up.\n\nKatara had heard from the Southern healers that the floodplain repair plan still depended on one inspection committee. She worried that decisions were being delayed because nobody wanted to take responsibility for the cost. Aang thought the committee needed time to hear witnesses. Toph believed several members were using the delay as an excuse not to acknowledge their own mistakes.\n\n\"I don't want them to rush,\" Katara said. \"I want them to make a decision.\"\n\nAang set down his cup.\n\n\"If they decide without hearing Sela, they'll repeat the problem.\"\n\n\"They have heard Sela.\"\n\n\"Not about the revised path.\"\n\nToph snorted.\n\n\"They've heard everyone. They just don't like the answer.\"\n\nI looked at the table.\n\nIroh was eating quietly. He wasn't going to rescue us from the discomfort.\n\n\"Can we agree on what needs to happen next?\" I asked.\n\n\"We need more evidence,\" Aang said.\n\n\"We need to use the evidence we have,\" Katara replied.\n\n\"We need a council that isn't afraid of dirt,\" Toph added.\n\n\"Toph.\"\n\n\"What? I can smell the fear.\"\n\nI could feel myself becoming the person who wanted to make everyone happy before dessert. It was a familiar reflex. If I could turn the argument into a tidy plan, maybe no one would go to bed upset.\n\nBut the question wasn't actually simple.\n\n\"Let's separate the issues,\" I said. \"Safety, timing, and accountability.\"\n\nAang had drawn a little bridge on the back of the supper menu. The bridge was smiling. He had given it eyebrows.\n\n\"Is that meant to persuade the council?\" Toph asked.\n\n\"It's meant to remind us there's a bridge on the other side of all these arguments.\"\n\n\"Looks more like a caterpillar.\"\n\nKatara set down her chopsticks. \"There's a family whose vegetable plot is flooded every time that bridge backs up. They won't find your caterpillar reassuring.\"\n\nAang's ears went pink. \"I know. I didn't mean—\"\n\n\"I know what you meant.\" She rubbed a hand over her face. The journey home had left her tired, and we'd hardly given her time to take off her coat. \"I just don't want to arrive tomorrow and find that we've spent another week discussing how to discuss it.\"\n\nToph leaned back. \"Then go talk to the family. Ask Sela where the water actually comes up. Don't make me stamp a plan I haven't walked.\"\n\nThe room went quiet. Aang flattened the menu in front of him.\n\n\"I want a hearing because people deserve a say,\" he said, more slowly. \"Not because I want to wait forever.\"\n\n\"And I want to protect them tonight,\" Katara answered, \"not make their choices for them for the next ten years.\"\n\nI stared at the dumplings cooling on the table. I knew all three of them loved one another in ways that weren't identical. I had told them separately that I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory. Somehow that made me especially tempted to turn every disagreement into proof we were doing relationships correctly.\n\nBut nobody had asked me to referee. I could love Katara's urgency, Aang's care and Toph's refusal to flatter an engineer without choosing one of them as tonight's winner.\n\n\"Can I make a suggestion that doesn't solve the bridge?\" I asked.\n\nToph grinned. \"Finally. A good suggestion.\"\n\n\"Let's eat before Katara has to catch another boat. Tomorrow we can write the questions we still need answered.\"\n\nKatara looked at me as though she'd forgotten food existed. Then she took a bite of dumpling and closed her eyes.\n\n\"That's disgusting,\" she said fondly. \"Who put so much pepper in these?\"\n\n\"Toph,\" Aang and I said together.\n\n\"You're welcome,\" Toph replied.\n\nWe talked for another twenty minutes. We did not reach a perfect agreement.\n\nKatara believed the current evidence justified temporary action to protect the gardens. Aang wanted the affected residents to sign off on the revised plan. Toph was willing to inspect temporary barriers but refused to let her assessment replace anyone else's testimony.\n\nThey were all making reasonable points.\n\nI was frustrated anyway.\n\nIroh finally set down his cup.\n\n\"I wonder,\" he said, \"whether tonight's dinner requires the council to have reached a decision.\"\n\nToph turned toward him.\n\n\"We're not going to solve the bridge by eating dumplings.\"\n\n\"No,\" he agreed. \"But perhaps we can decide which part of the conversation belongs to tomorrow.\"\n\nI looked at Katara.\n\nShe looked tired. Not just from travel, but from weeks of responsibility in a clinic where every decision had mattered. She'd come home, and we'd greeted her with another public problem.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" I said.\n\nShe frowned.\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For acting like you had to arrive and fix everything immediately.\"\n\nHer shoulders lowered.\n\n\"I don't mind talking about it. I mind that we keep turning every meal into a meeting.\"\n\nAang nodded.\n\n\"I do that.\"\n\n\"We all do,\" I said.\n\nToph took another dumpling.\n\n\"Speak for yourselves. I mostly turn meals into arguments about the food.\"\n\nKatara laughed.\n\nThe tension eased without vanishing.\n\nWe almost made it to dessert before Katara remembered the shipment she had promised the Southern clinic.\n\n\"Did the bandages go?\" she asked.\n\nAang and I looked at each other. He had been waiting for the revised list; I had assumed the delivery was already arranged. Toph made a noise suggesting she had been expecting precisely this mistake.\n\nKatara reached for her travel bag. I caught myself preparing to apologize and volunteer her for another hour of work.\n\n\"Can we see the list first?\" I asked.\n\nIt asked for two boxes of clean bandages, copies of the training notes, and a replacement stove valve. The local healers were already making their own treatment decisions. Nobody needed Katara to go back tomorrow and rescue them from an ordinary delivery.\n\n\"I can ask whether the Threadway keeper has a safe parcel window,\" Aang said.\n\n\"Ask,\" Toph repeated. \"Don't dump a job on them.\"\n\n\"Ask,\" Aang agreed.\n\nToph offered to check the valve measurements if someone brought them to her workshop. I wrote down the person responsible for each task so Katara didn't have to remember everything after a long journey.\n\nIroh regarded our makeshift list. \"You've turned dessert into a council meeting.\"\n\n\"We're remarkably gifted at it,\" I admitted.\n\n\"Then adjourn.\"\n\nWe did. Toph asked Katara what she'd enjoyed most while away, and Katara answered with a story about two young healers arguing over the best way to label a drawer. Aang confessed that his last kite had spun in circles. Toph requested a demonstration so she could make fun of it accurately.\n\nLater I asked what each of us wanted tomorrow. Aang wanted a walk, Toph an uninterrupted hour, Katara breakfast in her own home. We agreed on breakfast and left the rest open. Polyamory didn't mean flattening three people into one set of wants. It meant listening to the differences without making anybody compete for a place at the table.\n\n\nAfter dinner, Iroh helped dry the dishes. He didn't comment on our relationship structure, though he knew Jasper's commitments were openly non-monogamous and consensual. He had never treated our household as an interesting problem to solve. He simply washed the cups and asked where we kept the towels.\n\nWhen he left, Katara walked with me to the garden.\n\nThe night smelled of wet stone and the first flowers of the sea-glass orchard carried on the wind. I took her hand.\n\n\"Are you really back for a while?\" I asked.\n\n\"For now. The clinic can manage without me, and I need to remember that doesn't mean I failed them.\"\n\nI squeezed her fingers.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nShe looked at me.\n\n\"Did you miss me?\"\n\n\"Every day.\"\n\n\"And did you have a life while I was gone?\"\n\n\"Also every day.\"\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"Good. I want you to.\"\n\nI leaned closer.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nHer kiss was warm and familiar, but not the same as before she'd left. There was new tiredness in her shoulders, new steadiness in her hands. We had both changed a little.\n\nI liked that we could meet each other again.\n\nInside, Aang and Toph were arguing over the last dumpling. The argument ended when they split it.\n\nTomorrow we would return to the bridge. Tonight we closed the council notebook and made room at the table for the person who had come home.","word_count":1828},"love-and-duty-48":{"number":48,"title":"Water for the Other Side","content":"The spreadsheet said we had enough water.\n\nThe people waiting at the east end of the bridge said otherwise.\n\nI stared at the figures and felt my face grow warm. The table I'd made showed thirty containers delivered to Riverstone Terrace, twelve to the clinic, eight to Brasswatch Yard, and six to the temporary shelter on the ridge. The total matched the supply account.\n\nIt was beautiful arithmetic.\n\nIt was a terrible distribution.\n\nKatara stood beside me, reading the paper.\n\n\"How many people are at the east shelter?\" she asked.\n\n\"Eighteen.\"\n\n\"And at the west?\"\n\n\"Seven.\"\n\n\"How many containers?\"\n\n\"Six east. Ten west.\"\n\nShe tapped the page.\n\n\"There's our problem.\"\n\nThe bridge had remained closed while the supports were repaired and tested after the storm. People could use the longer ridge route, but not every family could carry heavy containers that distance. The supply team had delivered water to the places closest to the storage depot first, then sent what remained to the harder-to-reach shelter.\n\nNobody had set out to be cruel.\n\nThe process had been convenient for the people carrying the crates, not fair to those who needed them.\n\nI reached for my pencil.\n\n\"We can fix the numbers.\"\n\n\"Eventually,\" Katara said. \"But first we need to talk to the people who are short.\"\n\nWe walked to the east shelter with Aang. The wind was still strong along the canal, and the lower path smelled of wet stone. A temporary ramp led into the hall, built after Edda's access inspection.\n\nA woman named Sela greeted us at the door.\n\n\"Do you have water?\"\n\n\"Some,\" Katara said. \"Not enough yet. We're checking what everyone needs.\"\n\nSela pointed to a row of empty vessels.\n\n\"Some of us can drink from the spring if we carry it. Others can't carry the distance. The oldest person here needs water brought to her, and the family with the baby is boiling what they can find.\"\n\nKatara nodded.\n\n\"Then they go first.\"\n\nI wrote down the specific needs without putting private names on the public board.\n\nAang looked at the route.\n\n\"I could carry water across the canal with bending.\"\n\nSela looked at him.\n\n\"You could. What happens when you leave?\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"We need a system that doesn't depend on me.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI felt a small rush of admiration. He'd learned to hear that question without treating it as an insult.\n\nWe returned to the square and called the distribution volunteers together. Mara had been packing supplies, Danse had helped design the carrying frames, and Greg had offered a morning shift with the handcart. Toph stood near the wall, listening to the movement through the stone floor.\n\nI put the numbers on the board.\n\n\"These are the deliveries,\" I said. \"But this is not the same as the needs.\"\n\nOne volunteer frowned.\n\n\"We delivered to every district.\"\n\n\"Yes. We didn't deliver proportionately to the people who couldn't access the other supplies.\"\n\nAnother looked embarrassed.\n\n\"We used the closest route first.\"\n\n\"I know. That's why we're changing it.\"\n\nToph tapped the floor.\n\n\"Your chart counts containers. It doesn't count the distance a person has to drag them.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nWe made a new plan with actual requirements. Katara confirmed the clinic's medical supply needs. Sela arranged a contact person at the east shelter without making the residents' private details public. Danse checked the handcart load. Edda tested the route so the deliveries wouldn't block the accessible path.\n\nAang offered to carry the heaviest containers for the first round, with two local volunteers learning the handling process for later shifts.\n\nNo one was assigned more than they'd agreed to do.\n\nThe revised plan meant the west shelter would receive fewer extra containers for one day. Some residents complained. They had been relieved to have a reserve and did not like hearing that it had been allocated unevenly.\n\nI couldn't blame them for feeling anxious.\n\nI could still tell the truth.\n\n\"We're not taking away your minimum needs,\" I explained. \"We're correcting a distribution that left another group without theirs.\"\n\nA man near the depot folded his arms.\n\n\"Easy for you to say.\"\n\nHe was right about one thing: I was not the person who would have to walk farther for water if the plan failed.\n\n\"Then help us test it,\" I said.\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n\"We need your experience of the route. And if you find a problem, I want to know.\"\n\nHe did.\n\nWe took the draft to the east shelter before asking anyone to call it fair. A woman named Amel was distributing cups near the entrance. She held the sheet with two fingers, as though it might leave ink on her hands.\n\n\"Thirty containers,\" she read. \"For the whole terrace?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"How many of those are supposed to arrive before the night shift starts?\"\n\nI checked. \"Twelve.\"\n\nShe tapped a line with her nail. \"We have six people working outside until dark. They won't be here to collect water at that hour.\"\n\nI was ready to explain the schedule, then stopped. The schedule was mine. The people waiting for water hadn't agreed to live according to it.\n\n\"What time would actually work?\" I asked.\n\nShe wrote two possible windows in the margin, then asked whether the depot could hold a few containers for people who preferred not to explain their household arrangements in public. Katara said the shelter could record quantities without publishing names. That was different from hiding how much water it had received.\n\nAang wanted to help with deliveries. Amel looked at his glider.\n\n\"Not everything can arrive from the sky,\" she said. \"Some people need to know what happens when you're not here.\"\n\nHe winced, then nodded. \"You're right.\"\n\nToph tested the stone lip of the north path with her foot. \"And the cart still has to turn a corner. Which brilliant person drew that?\"\n\nI raised my hand.\n\n\"Of course you did.\"\n\nI laughed despite the heat in my cheeks. She wasn't gentle about an error, but she didn't make me afraid to change it. There was affection in her bluntness, and I wasn't required to enjoy it every minute to know she cared.\n\nWhen the sheet finally had a plan that Amel could use, I passed her the pen. She signed the receipt for the proposed time, not for water that had yet to arrive.\n\n\"Come back when the carts do,\" she said.\n\nWe did. The plan still needed a driver's eye before it could become a real route.\n\nThe first revision nearly failed at the depot.\n\nA cart driver named Beren looked at the new sheet and shook his head.\n\n\"You've moved the east delivery earlier, but you're still using the narrow lane by the canal. My cart can't turn around there when the rain comes.\"\n\nI studied the map. He was right. In my hurry to fix the unequal water totals, I had reused the old route without checking whether the carriers could actually make the journey.\n\n\"Which path would you use?\" I asked.\n\n\"The north path. Longer, but the stones are solid.\"\n\nToph tapped her foot against the ground.\n\n\"He's right about the surface.\"\n\n\"Can Edda check the turns?\" Katara asked.\n\n\"Already did yesterday,\" Beren said. \"I watched her. She made me move a stack of crates so she could pass.\"\n\nI looked up.\n\n\"Thank you for doing that.\"\n\n\"She didn't ask politely.\"\n\nToph grinned. \"Sounds like Edda.\"\n\nWe changed the route.\n\nAang offered to carry the heaviest containers along the first run, but Sela reminded him that the local volunteers needed practice managing the handcart without bending. They divided the loads by what people could safely handle. Aang worked with them, not instead of them.\n\nAt the east shelter, a woman asked whether the delivery record would name the people who received extra water.\n\n\"No,\" Katara said. \"The public record will show how much arrived and what needs it served. Your medical or family details aren't for the notice board.\"\n\nThe woman nodded and accepted the containers.\n\nI crossed out another line on the spreadsheet. The new table looked worse than the old one. It had arrows, corrections, and a reminder to review the distribution after two days.\n\nIt was also the first version that people who used the routes had helped write.\n\nI had spent so long worrying about being a responsible member of our complicated household that I'd forgotten the same principle mattered outside our door. Caring for people wasn't making decisions for them. It was asking which decisions actually changed the day they had to live.\n\nThe second delivery went better. The east shelter received its immediate supplies before evening. The west retained enough for confirmed needs. The public ledger showed the changes and the reasons, without turning vulnerable households into a list of people to be judged.\n\nWe returned home exhausted.\n\nKatara washed the dust from her hands and looked at my spreadsheet, now covered in corrections.\n\n\"Do you still like it?\" she asked.\n\n\"Not particularly.\"\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"Good. That means it's being used.\"\n\nToph sat at the table with a bowl of soup.\n\n\"Spreadsheets are like people,\" she announced. \"They get annoying when they think they're always right.\"\n\nAang laughed.\n\nI sat beside her.\n\n\"Are you comparing me to a spreadsheet?\"\n\n\"Only on your worst days.\"\n\nKatara came around the table and rested a hand on my shoulder.\n\n\"You noticed a problem,\" she said. \"Then you listened. That's what matters.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\n\"We still missed it for two days.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nShe didn't soften the truth.\n\nThen she kissed my forehead.\n\n\"We'll do better tomorrow.\"\n\nLater, when the dishes were done, Aang showed me the new route he'd drawn. It included shaded rest points, safe crossing times, and notes from the people who used the path daily. His handwriting was crooked from trying to fit too much information onto one page.\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"It looks terrible.\"\n\n\"It works.\"\n\n\"That might be the new standard.\"\n\nHe grinned and leaned close.\n\n\"Then perhaps I can ask for something that doesn't fit in a table.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"A quiet walk with you.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\nWe went outside beneath the lanterns.\n\nThe bridge remained closed. The water was still moving. The arithmetic was still imperfect.\n\nBut the people on the other side had water, and we'd stopped confusing a balanced column with a fair day.","word_count":1727}},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-44":{"number":44,"title":"A Door Without a Lock","content":"Danse had installed three new locks by breakfast.\n\nI discovered this when I tried to bring a crate of replacement fittings into the Brasswatch workshop and couldn't open the side door.\n\nI knocked.\n\nNothing.\n\nI knocked harder.\n\nDanse appeared on the other side, carrying a wrench.\n\n\"You're early,\" he said.\n\n\"The door is locked.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"With three locks.\"\n\n\"Two locks and a secondary latch.\"\n\n\"That is a very helpful distinction.\"\n\nHe took the crate before I dropped it, then stepped aside to let me in.\n\nThe workshop smelled of oil, clean metal, and the faint dry heat of the furnace. On the back bench lay the repaired purifier parts, still waiting for final clearance. Ash had left a note asking whether the shelf we'd fixed could hold heavier supplies. Pip had placed a basket of bread beside the window.\n\nEverything looked normal except the door.\n\n\"Why?\" I asked.\n\nDanse set down the crate.\n\n\"Security.\"\n\n\"Against what?\"\n\n\"Unauthorized entry.\"\n\n\"Who is authorized?\"\n\nHe looked at me as if the answer were obvious.\n\n\"People with permission.\"\n\n\"How do they get permission if the door is locked and you can't hear them?\"\n\nHe glanced toward the window. For the first time, he seemed to notice that the new latch sat above shoulder height for several people who used the workshop.\n\n\"It needs a bell,\" he said.\n\n\"It needs a plan.\"\n\nWe had agreed to make the workshop accessible after Edda's comments at the bridge meeting. Ash had asked for a room that could lock from the inside without requiring anyone to explain their identity. Tamsin wanted to be sure repairs wouldn't block the emergency exit. Danse had focused on keeping everyone safe from the possibility of a hostile visitor.\n\nHis solution made half the ordinary visitors feel as though they were approaching a checkpoint.\n\n\"I don't want to make this place unsafe,\" he said.\n\n\"Neither do I.\"\n\n\"Then why are you criticizing the locks?\"\n\n\"Because safety is more than keeping people out.\"\n\nHe rubbed his forehead.\n\n\"I know that in theory.\"\n\n\"Then let's test it in practice.\"\n\nWe invited Edda, Tamsin, Pip, and Ash to look at the entrance before any more work began. Danse was uncomfortable with so many people examining something he'd built. He did not complain.\n\nEdda arrived first.\n\nShe looked at the raised threshold, the narrow turn inside the door, and the three different mechanisms controlling the lock.\n\n\"How do I get through?\" she asked.\n\nDanse stepped forward.\n\n\"I can assist.\"\n\n\"That's not what I asked.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nShe measured the opening with a folding ruler.\n\n\"The door swings inward. If somebody's already standing by the first bench, there's no clear room. I can't open the top latch from my chair. And if there's a fire, I'm not waiting for the tall man with the keys.\"\n\nDanse wrote it down.\n\nAsh studied the side window.\n\n\"Can anyone see inside the private workroom from here?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" Danse said.\n\n\"Then it isn't private.\"\n\nHe wrote that down too.\n\nTamsin tapped the door frame.\n\n\"Your reinforcing plate blocks the emergency release.\"\n\nDanse looked genuinely startled.\n\n\"It shouldn't.\"\n\n\"It does.\"\n\nPip, who had come to the workshop many times to deliver supplies, asked how someone with their hands full was supposed to announce themselves.\n\nDanse looked at the bell rope he'd begun installing.\n\n\"We need to adjust the design.\"\n\n\"Quite a bit,\" I said.\n\nHe gave me a look.\n\n\"Yes. Quite a bit.\"\n\nBy lunch, we had a list of requirements instead of a collection of assumptions. The public workshop needed a wide, low-effort entrance, level approach, clear sightlines, and an accessible bell or signaling panel. The private repair room needed a lock controlled by the person inside, plus a way to request help that didn't reveal the reason for the request.\n\nThe storage area could remain secured, but only staff needed to use it. The emergency exit could not depend on finding Danse.\n\nWe drew the revised layout on the big worktable.\n\nHe measured each doorway twice.\n\nI liked watching him work when he'd stopped trying to be perfect. His concentration made a small line between his brows. He didn't talk much. When he disagreed, he pointed to the specific measurement instead of invoking rank.\n\nAsh came over from the private workroom carrying the old latch in a paper bag. She set it on the table so carefully that even Danse noticed.\n\n\"Can I have a different one?\" she asked.\n\nHe looked at the metal. \"It's still functional.\"\n\n\"From your side of the door.\"\n\nHe opened his mouth, then shut it. I could see him measuring the distance to the inside handle in his head. He'd spent the morning checking width and clearance; he hadn't asked how the mechanism felt to someone who needed to close it without calling a stronger person over.\n\nAsh showed him. She was shorter than he was, and when she turned the stiff catch her wrist bent awkwardly. She tried again before he could offer to do it for her.\n\n\"I can work here,\" she said. \"I just don't want the price of privacy to be a lock I can't use.\"\n\nDanse's jaw tightened. For a heartbeat I thought he would defend the design because he'd built it. Instead he took a pencil and wrote *operable by occupant* beside the drawing.\n\n\"Will you choose the replacement?\" he asked.\n\n\"I'd like to try the options first.\"\n\n\"Very well.\"\n\nI watched him cross out the old lock without striking through the whole plan. The thing he'd made wasn't worthless because someone found a flaw in it. It was simply unfinished.\n\nAsh looked at me. \"Does he always look like he's been sentenced when someone asks for a change?\"\n\n\"Only when it involves hardware.\"\n\nDanse gave me a long, injured look.\n\n\"Untrue,\" he said. \"I have accepted several changes to the lighting plan.\"\n\n\"After arguing about all of them.\"\n\n\"Those were technical discussions.\"\n\nAsh laughed, the sound bright in the workshop. Danse didn't smile, but the furrow between his brows eased.\n\nLater, while Edda prepared the ramp test, he stood beside me and brushed my fingers with his. He rarely made a performance out of tenderness. The small movement had all the weight of him saying he wanted me close without deciding that I needed to be.\n\n\"I should have asked her first,\" he said.\n\n\"You did ask her now.\"\n\n\"After she had to ask me.\"\n\n\"Then remember it next time.\"\n\nHe nodded. I didn't give him an absolution he hadn't requested. We had a ramp to test and a new latch to find.\n\nEdda tested the first temporary ramp that afternoon.\n\nIt was too steep.\n\nDanse looked dismayed.\n\n\"Let's change the angle,\" she said.\n\n\"That will require moving the outer step.\"\n\n\"Then move it.\"\n\nHe knelt beside the stone.\n\n\"Right.\"\n\nThe second attempt was better, but the turning space was still too narrow. Tamsin helped shift a bench. Ash built a folding shelf so supplies could be set down without blocking the route.\n\nAt dusk, we opened the door for another test.\n\nEdda entered without waiting for anyone to carry her over the threshold.\n\nDanse was silent.\n\n\"Now,\" she said, \"we need to make sure it still works when it rains.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Tomorrow.\"\n\nI thought he might be disappointed that there was no neat ending.\n\nInstead he seemed relieved to have a real next step.\n\nAfter the others left, he stood beneath the new bell cord.\n\n\"I thought I was protecting people,\" he said.\n\n\"You were trying to.\"\n\n\"That's not the same.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe looked toward the private room, where Ash had asked us not to install an external viewing panel.\n\n\"In the Brotherhood, everything began with securing the perimeter. After that, we decided who was allowed inside.\"\n\n\"And here?\"\n\n\"Here the people who use the place get to help decide what safe means.\"\n\nI leaned against the workbench.\n\n\"That seems like progress.\"\n\nHe studied me.\n\n\"You don't have to reassure me.\"\n\n\"I wasn't. I was observing.\"\n\nHe smiled faintly.\n\n\"You're getting good at that.\"\n\nHe took the last tool from the bench and put it away. The movements were slower than usual. I could see fatigue settling in the way he held his shoulders.\n\n\"Come home,\" I said.\n\n\"I still need to check the hinges.\"\n\n\"Tomorrow.\"\n\nHe looked tempted to argue.\n\nThen he set the wrench down.\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nOutside, the lanterns along Brasswatch Yard were coming on one by one. The air smelled of damp stone and bread from the square. He took my hand when I offered it.\n\nAt the door, he stopped.\n\n\"Can I ask you something?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"If I build a place where people can leave without asking permission, will they still come back?\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"Some will. Some won't. That's what makes coming back a choice.\"\n\nHe looked toward the open entrance.\n\n\"That is not a comfortable answer.\"\n\n\"No. But it's an honest one.\"\n\nHe lifted my hand and kissed it, then leaned close enough for me to feel the warmth of his breath.\n\n\"I want you to come back,\" he said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Is that too much to ask?\"\n\n\"No. As long as it's a request.\"\n\nHe smiled, and I kissed him beneath the new bell cord. We went home before the rain arrived.\n\nThe following morning, the workshop was open at its usual hour. The old top latch had been removed. The private room had a lock Ash could operate from inside, and the emergency exit opened with one motion.\n\nThe place was not finished.\n\nBut it was beginning to belong to the people who needed it, not just the man who had built the locks.","word_count":1609},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-48":{"number":48,"title":"The Night the Lights Went Out","content":"The generator failed with a sound like a very large person clearing their throat.\n\nThen the entire eastern side of Lanternwell Square went dark.\n\nI was at Brasswatch Yard with Danse, checking the volunteer repair schedule. One moment the workshop lights were humming; the next they were gone. Outside, somebody shouted. A few seconds later the emergency lantern by the door came on.\n\nDanse was already moving.\n\n\"Stay back from the panel,\" he said.\n\n\"I wasn't planning to touch it.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nHe opened the insulated access cover and looked at the gauges. The smell of overheated wiring hung in the air, sharp and unpleasant. The generator had been carrying extra load since the storm flooded the lower service route. We knew it needed inspection. We hadn't expected it to fail tonight.\n\nA runner arrived from the square.\n\n\"The hall's dark. People are sheltering from the weather.\"\n\nDanse looked at the panel.\n\n\"I can restore power.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"Not until I know what failed.\"\n\nThat was the right answer, though it hurt to hear it.\n\nWe brought the portable lanterns to the hall. Maui was already carrying blankets to the west benches. Katara was checking that the children had their caregivers nearby. Neri had a paper list of people who had chosen to wait there, with no public record of private destinations.\n\nI helped set lanterns where they wouldn't block the exit.\n\nDanse spoke to the volunteers.\n\n\"We need a trained person at the generator, two people to watch the backup lamps, and someone to check the cables for water damage. No one enters the electrical room without protective equipment.\"\n\nHe didn't assign people by pointing at them. He asked who was trained and available.\n\nRenn raised a hand.\n\n\"I can check the cable run. I trained on that system last week.\"\n\nDanse looked at them.\n\n\"With me.\"\n\nAster volunteered for the backup lamps. Pip offered to maintain the clear path, then asked to be relieved after an hour.\n\nEverything moved more slowly than a military emergency would have.\n\nIt also moved with people who knew what they'd agreed to do.\n\nWe found the fault near the old canal service junction. Water had reached an improperly sealed conduit. One breaker had tripped, and the generator's load control had tried to compensate until it shut down.\n\nDanse stared at the panel.\n\n\"I approved that conduit inspection last month.\"\n\n\"Was it completed?\" I asked.\n\nHe checked the log.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nI felt a cold knot in my stomach.\n\n\"Then we document that.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nThere would be time later for accountability. Right now we needed safe lights, dry equipment, and a plan that didn't make the next repair more dangerous than the outage itself.\n\nRenn tested the replacement cable while Danse checked the voltage. Aster reported that two backup lamps were losing power. We moved the remaining lights to the highest-traffic routes and made sure people knew which corridors were still dark.\n\nNo one was ordered to stay in the hall.\n\nNo one was forced through a Threadway.\n\nNear midnight, a deep note sounded from the direction of Tideglass. The second bell.\n\nDanse looked toward the door.\n\n\"Pressure in the lower route,\" he said.\n\n\"Could it affect the generator?\"\n\n\"Possibly. We should assume the service junction is still wet until we test it.\"\n\nHe refused to restart the system immediately.\n\nA few people complained.\n\nOne man shouted that the old Brotherhood would have had the lights back already.\n\nDanse looked at him.\n\n\"The old Brotherhood might have ordered someone into a flooded electrical room. That doesn't make it safe.\"\n\nThe man stopped.\n\nI watched Danse turn back to the panel.\n\nIt was a small victory, but one he'd earned with work rather than a speech.\n\nAt two in the morning Aster returned from the shelter carrying the last charged emergency lamp.\n\n\"One of the halls is too dark,\" they said. \"The lanterns are bright enough near the main door, but the side passage doesn't have any light.\"\n\nDanse checked the diagram.\n\n\"We could move one from the equipment room.\"\n\nRenn shook their head. \"We need that light to see the shutdown indicator if the backup system fails.\"\n\nI looked between them.\n\n\"Then what's the other option?\"\n\nNobody answered immediately.\n\nRenn brought the load sheet over from the generator panel. In the light of the emergency lantern, the numbers looked like little black insects crawling down the page.\n\n\"We can keep the clinic refrigerator on backup,\" they said. \"Not the repair presses. Not tonight.\"\n\nDanse took the sheet. \"The press line could be shut down by section.\"\n\n\"It already is,\" Renn answered. \"You don't need to go back and switch it off a second time.\"\n\nI watched his expression. He hated being told he was too late to solve something, even when the problem had been solved well.\n\nAster came in from the hall with a damp coat and a question about the east exit. Someone had moved a spare crate to make space for a lantern, and now the route was too narrow for the cart carrying drinking water.\n\nDanse turned toward the door.\n\nRenn stopped him. \"I'll take the panel. You can check the hall with Aster. One problem at a time.\"\n\nHe hesitated, then handed back the sheet.\n\nI followed him into the corridor. The floor felt colder without the workshop lights. In the dark a small mistake seemed enormous; people were whispering, and someone had dropped a spoon that rang across the tiles like a bell.\n\n\"We've trained for power loss,\" Danse said under his breath.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why do I feel as though I'm improvising?\"\n\n\"Because this is happening to real people, not a training map.\"\n\nHe stared at the crate and helped Aster move it clear. The work was ordinary and unglamorous. He could have thrown the box aside without breaking a sweat, but he waited while Aster checked where it belonged. Renn was still monitoring the generator. Nobody needed him to command both places at once.\n\n\"Is this enough?\" he asked me.\n\n\"To make the path clear? Let's ask the person who uses it.\"\n\nWe did. The cart passed without catching the wall. Aster wrote the clearance in the log, and Danse looked less interested in being the first to claim the crisis was over.\n\nWhen he returned to the panel, Renn had new readings. He bent over the figures and listened.\n\nPip arrived with a sketch of the hall showing the two routes people were actually using. One led to the kitchens; the other went to the sheltered waiting rooms. The lamp beside the closed storage door had been placed there because the old plan showed it as an entrance.\n\n\"Could we move that one?\" I asked.\n\nPip nodded. \"The storage door's locked and signed. Nobody needs to go through it tonight.\"\n\nDanse considered.\n\n\"Provided the exit markings remain visible.\"\n\nThey walked the route together before moving anything. Aster carried the lamp, Renn checked the cable was clear of the footpath, and Pip tested whether the light could be seen from the hall's far corner.\n\nAn older traveler asked whether they could still leave by the eastern route.\n\n\"Yes,\" Pip said. \"It is open if you choose to use it. We can show you the map.\"\n\nThe traveler chose to wait until morning instead.\n\nNo one argued.\n\nBack at the yard, Danse wrote the temporary arrangement on the board and assigned a recheck before dawn.\n\n\"This system needs a permanent backup,\" he said.\n\n\"With a budget and a maintenance schedule,\" I added.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Naturally.\"\n\nI took his hand briefly.\n\nHe was still learning the hardest part: a good emergency plan did not depend on him staying awake forever. It depended on other people being able to follow it when he went home.\n\nThe backup power held until dawn. When the water level fell, the team completed the insulation test, replaced the damaged seal, and restarted the generator at reduced load. Lights returned to the workshop first, then the square.\n\nThere was no applause.\n\nThere was a list of repairs.\n\nWe posted the temporary load limit, the inspection results, and the next scheduled test. Renn added their signature beside the work they'd actually performed. Danse added his to the line documenting the missed conduit inspection.\n\nBy the time the last lantern was collected, my hands were shaking with exhaustion.\n\nDanse noticed.\n\n\"You're cold.\"\n\n\"I'm tired.\"\n\n\"Then come home.\"\n\n\"There's still work.\"\n\n\"There's always work.\"\n\nHe said it with such quiet conviction that I looked up.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"Renn and Aster have the next shift. Tamsin will review the repair. I've left the equipment in a safe state.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\nWe walked to his room above the workshop. The familiar smell of clean metal and dry wood met me as he opened the door. He set the toolbelt aside, took off his coat, and stood for a moment looking at the lamp on the table.\n\n\"I used to feel better only when the lights came back,\" he said.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I feel better knowing nobody was hurt because I wanted to restore them too quickly.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"That matters.\"\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n\"Do you want to stay?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Because you want to, not because it's late?\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nHe laughed softly.\n\nHis kiss was careful at first, then warmer when I rested my hands against his chest. We stood close enough to hear the rain easing outside. No one needed a heroic performance from us.\n\nWhen he asked if I wanted to go inside, I said yes.\n\nWe closed the door and left the rest of the night private.\n\nIn the morning, there was a note from Renn.\n\nThe generator had passed its second test.\n\nUnderneath, in smaller writing, they had added: THANK YOU FOR NOT RUSHING.\n\nDanse read it twice.\n\nThen he folded it and put it in his pocket.","word_count":1656}},"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-47":{"number":47,"title":"The Night After the Show","content":"The last cable had tangled itself around Greg's ankle.\n\nThat was the only explanation I could find for why a man who had spent decades handling concert equipment was standing in the middle of Starwake Pier looking personally betrayed by a length of black cord.\n\n\"I swear it wasn't like that when I put it down,\" he said.\n\nI looked at the knot.\n\n\"Maybe the cable has a private life.\"\n\n\"Very funny.\"\n\n\"It could be ethically non-monogamous. Perhaps it's seeing the speaker cable and the microphone stand.\"\n\nHe laughed so hard he had to lean against the equipment case.\n\n\"Don't tell me you're jealous of a cable.\"\n\n\"Only of how much attention you're giving it.\"\n\nHe grinned and reached for my hand, but the cable tightened around his boot.\n\n\"Hold that thought.\"\n\nI bent down and unwound the loop.\n\nThe Listening Fair had ended an hour earlier. The crowd was gone, the tea table had been cleaned, and the donation box had been counted with Mara present. The temporary lights were off except for the safety lanterns along the accessible path.\n\nWe had agreed to finish the cleanup before leaving.\n\nGreg had agreed very enthusiastically.\n\nNow he was trying to make an enemy out of electrical equipment.\n\n\"Maybe you should write a song about it,\" I said.\n\n\"I already have three verses.\"\n\n\"Of course you do.\"\n\nHe hummed something ridiculous as we packed the cables. I joined in with a deliberately awful harmony. His smile grew until he couldn't keep the melody straight.\n\nIt was the kind of evening I liked best with him. No performance required, no audience expecting us to turn love into a single perfect story. Just the two of us, a lot of equipment, and the small satisfaction of work actually finished.\n\nWe carried the last case to the storage shed.\n\nI checked the lock.\n\nGreg checked it again.\n\n\"You're learning from Danse,\" I said.\n\n\"Hey, his system works.\"\n\n\"Only after we removed the part that needed a ladder.\"\n\nGreg laughed.\n\nWe started toward the Starwake Door.\n\nThe night air was cool enough that I pulled my coat tighter. He noticed without making a fuss and offered me the spare scarf he'd brought. It was faded blue, soft from years of use, and smelled faintly of laundry soap.\n\n\"Thanks,\" I said.\n\n\"Always.\"\n\nHe slipped an arm around my shoulders as we walked.\n\nFor a while we didn't talk. The sound of the sea moved beneath the pier. Lanterns glowed along the path back to Lanternwell Square, where a few late visitors were making their way home from the other districts.\n\nThen Greg stopped.\n\n\"Can I ask you something?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"Are you happy?\"\n\nThe question was so simple that I didn't know how to answer it.\n\n\"Right now?\"\n\n\"Yeah. Right now.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"I'm tired. My feet hurt. I have a notebook full of repairs we still need to schedule. And I'm happy.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Are you?\"\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nHe looked toward the dark water.\n\n\"I used to think happy meant getting to the part after everything difficult. The concert's over, the money's enough, everybody's okay, nobody's upset.\"\n\n\"That sounds exhausting.\"\n\n\"It was.\"\n\nHe rubbed his thumb over my knuckles.\n\n\"Now I think it can just be this. A night where we did something good and still have a few problems to deal with tomorrow.\"\n\nI leaned closer.\n\n\"That's a very nice way to say we have chores.\"\n\n\"Romantic, right?\"\n\n\"Unbelievably.\"\n\nHe kissed my cheek.\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"Was that all you had planned?\"\n\nHe raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"I could do better.\"\n\n\"Could you?\"\n\n\"Challenge accepted.\"\n\nHe kissed me properly this time, warm and slow, the kind that made the cold air feel less important. His hand rested lightly at my waist. I held the edge of his jacket and smiled against his mouth.\n\nWhen we parted, he looked a little breathless.\n\n\"Better?\"\n\n\"Much.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\nNola caught us before we left. Her sleeves were pushed to her elbows, and she'd been carrying the same clipboard since the first soundcheck.\n\n\"Greg, you're done,\" she said.\n\n\"I am aware.\"\n\n\"Then stop taking cables out of other people's hands.\"\n\nHe blinked at the coil he'd just reached for. \"I thought I was helping.\"\n\n\"You are. But Lio's crew is paid for cleanup and you have an actual life to go home to.\"\n\nGreg looked at me, his hair falling into his eyes. \"Do I?\"\n\n\"Allegedly.\"\n\nNola checked a box on the sheet. \"Also, your guitar case is in the wrong storage bay.\"\n\n\"You're destroying my reputation.\"\n\n\"The case weighs more than the small instrument shelf can safely hold.\"\n\nHe took it without arguing. I loved that his first response was a joke and his second was to fix the problem. Not every lesson needed a solemn speech.\n\nOutside, we stood under the covered path while a drizzle began to spot the lantern glass. Greg watched the crew finish rolling the last cable without him. He looked oddly lonely for a moment.\n\n\"I always used to stay until the last person left,\" he said. \"Then I could tell myself I'd been necessary.\"\n\n\"Did it work?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. Mostly it meant I was too tired to enjoy the ride home.\"\n\nHe tucked his guitar safely under the awning. \"You know I'm still gonna worry when you travel to see the others.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"But you've told me you're ethically non-monogamous and you prefer polyamory. I don't want to make you prove you care by missing your own plans.\"\n\nI caught the front of his jacket, tugging him close enough for our foreheads to touch.\n\n\"You can tell me you miss me,\" I said. \"I don't mind. I just don't want missing me to become a verdict.\"\n\nHe smiled. \"That's the least catchy song title I ever heard.\"\n\n\"It'll be a hit.\"\n\nHe hummed a deliberately dreadful chorus under his breath. I laughed, and he kissed the corner of my mouth before asking whether I wanted to go back to Beach City. I did. Nola's clipboard remained at Starwake, in the hands of somebody who knew exactly where the cables belonged.\n\nWe went through the Starwake Door into Beach City, where the van waited beneath a streetlamp. The familiar smell of the coast met me: salt, asphalt, and somebody's late-night food from a nearby shop.\n\nGreg opened the van and set down his guitar.\n\n\"Want to stay?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked at me for another moment.\n\n\"Because you want to?\"\n\n\"Because I want to.\"\n\nHe smiled, relief and affection moving together across his face.\n\nInside, we folded the blanket, put the equipment list on the dashboard, and made a small space where there had been too many things. Greg found a packet of crackers and offered them with great ceremony.\n\n\"A feast.\"\n\n\"Truly, you spoil me.\"\n\n\"I try.\"\n\nWe ate the crackers and laughed about the fair. He imitated Toph declaring the practice platform \"almost competent.\" I tried to imitate Iroh's calm reply and failed because I started laughing halfway through the sentence.\n\nThen the laughter settled.\n\nGreg reached for my hand.\n\n\"I know you have other people you love,\" he said quietly. \"I know we talked about ethical non-monogamy and polyamory, and I know it doesn't make tonight less ours.\"\n\nI squeezed his fingers.\n\n\"It doesn't.\"\n\n\"I just wanted to say that I like having nights that belong to us because we choose them.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe leaned close.\n\nThe next kiss was unhurried. There was no need to fill the silence with a joke, though I knew he would if I asked. I rested my forehead against his and listened to the small sounds of the van cooling in the night.\n\n\"Stay with me,\" he whispered.\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nWe turned down the light and let the rest of the evening be private.\n\nIn the morning, sunlight came through the windshield and woke me before the alarm. Greg was still asleep, one hand resting near the folded equipment list. His hair looked as though it had been involved in a disagreement with a pillow.\n\nI smiled.\n\nI was tempted to let him sleep.\n\nThen I remembered that the repair team was expecting the donation total by nine.\n\nI nudged his shoulder.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\nA muffled sound.\n\n\"Greg, we have responsibilities.\"\n\n\"Tell them I'm a guitar.\"\n\n\"That makes no sense.\"\n\n\"Great guitars don't do paperwork.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe opened one eye.\n\n\"Coffee?\"\n\n\"After the totals are sent.\"\n\n\"Brutal.\"\n\nWe made coffee anyway, then sat together at the small table outside the van and checked the ledger. The donations covered the new fasteners and some of the lumber, exactly as we'd announced. We sent the figures to Tamsin and copied the volunteers who had helped. No exaggeration. No promise that the rest of the repairs would happen by magic.\n\nGreg looked at the final number.\n\n\"Not everything we wanted.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Enough for the next step.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe leaned over and kissed my temple.\n\n\"Good morning.\"\n\n\"Good morning.\"\n\nBy the time we returned to Starwake Pier, the volunteers were already moving supplies to the safe storage area. Toph had left a note about checking the platform after the next rain. Iroh had sent a small tin of the new house tea blend as thanks.\n\nWe got to work.\n\nThat was the best part of the night after the show: the morning wasn't a punishment for being happy.\n\nIt was simply the next part of our life.","word_count":1573},"su-49":{"number":49,"title":"The Note Below the Water","content":"The empty stage made a sound.\n\nI heard it while we were checking the lanterns along Starwake Pier. It was a low note, almost musical, vibrating through the wooden platform after a gust of wind moved across the water.\n\nGreg stopped.\n\n\"Did you hear that?\"\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"Was that your guitar?\"\n\nHe held up both hands.\n\n\"Nope.\"\n\nThe stage was empty. The cables had been packed away after the Listening Fair, and the repaired railings were still marked with temporary load limits. The western section had not yet reopened.\n\nThe note sounded again.\n\nIt was deeper than anything the little stage should have produced.\n\nGreg crouched near the platform.\n\n\"That's coming from underneath.\"\n\nI thought of the second bell at Tideglass.\n\n\"Can we check safely?\"\n\nHe looked at the railing.\n\n\"Not from the closed section. Let's get someone who knows the structure.\"\n\nWe found Tamsin near the storage shed. She listened to our description, then asked us to repeat exactly where we'd stood and what the wind had been doing. She was not interested in a ghost story.\n\nDanse arrived with a vibration meter.\n\nToph came because Tamsin had asked whether the stone support beneath the pier was moving.\n\nGreg looked between them.\n\n\"I think I know what note it is.\"\n\nToph raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"That's useful, but I need to know what's making it.\"\n\n\"Fair.\"\n\nWe set up the meter on the safe platform and waited for the wind to shift.\n\nThe note came again.\n\nDanse recorded the frequency. Toph felt the slight movement through the stone support. Tamsin checked the old service map. The measurements suggested a cavity beneath the pier was resonating when water passed through a narrow opening.\n\n\"That could be connected to the old drainage route,\" I said.\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Possibly. We need a water-level comparison before we say more.\"\n\nGreg tapped the edge of the stage.\n\n\"It's like a guitar body. The wood makes one part of the sound, the hollow space makes another.\"\n\nToph grinned.\n\n\"Finally, the musician says something useful.\"\n\n\"High praise.\"\n\nWe laughed.\n\nThen the wind shifted and the note grew louder.\n\nFen sent a warning from Tideglass. The tide was rising faster than the morning chart had predicted. The lower service passage was still sealed, and several surface drains were carrying more water than usual.\n\nTamsin looked at the stage.\n\n\"Close this section.\"\n\nWe moved the lanterns away from the western boards and posted a clear route to the upper path. Greg helped carry the equipment, checking with the volunteers rather than assigning jobs on the spot. Neri updated the public notice without listing any traveler's private destination.\n\nThe storm had not arrived yet.\n\nThat was why we had time.\n\nBefore sunset, the volunteer crew went over the evacuation plan one more time. A younger helper named Jo wanted to know whether we ought to warn everyone who lived near the water that the old tunnel was opening.\n\n\"It's not opening,\" Fen said from the speaking station. \"The surface drains are handling the extra flow. The lower historic passage is still sealed.\"\n\nJo looked relieved, then embarrassed. \"I told my brother there was a secret tunnel under the stage.\"\n\n\"Well,\" Greg said, \"there might be a very boring maintenance route. I'm not volunteering to write a song about the plumbing.\"\n\nI laughed, but Danse was checking the measurements with such a serious face that I stopped.\n\n\"Does the sound mean something's going to fail?\" I asked.\n\n\"We don't know yet. It might mean air is moving through a cavity. It might mean the pressure is changing. The point is to observe without treating the noise as an instruction to go inside.\"\n\nTamsin recorded the warning limits and asked Jo to help point people toward the upper stairs, where the signs had been tested at eye level for several kinds of visitors. Jo said yes. There were no new volunteers sent onto the closed platform after that.\n\nThe lower note came again while Danse was adjusting the recorder, and Greg made a face I recognized from rehearsal.\n\n\"What?\" I asked.\n\n\"Nothing. It's just not where I expected it.\"\n\n\"You mean the pitch?\"\n\n\"Yeah. It almost sounds like the note wants to go somewhere else.\"\n\nDanse looked up. \"That is not a measurement.\"\n\nGreg raised his hands. \"Didn't say it was.\"\n\nFor a second I thought they'd start an argument over the stage. Instead Greg asked to hear the recording through the safe monitor. Jo checked with Tamsin before playing it back. The low hum was thin without the wind, and I could hear something else under it: the squeak of the old rail where the boards flexed.\n\n\"It sounds worse recorded,\" I said.\n\n\"More useful,\" Danse answered.\n\nGreg turned to him. \"Could you mark the time where the squeak starts? I want to know what my ear was trying to follow.\"\n\nDanse studied him. \"For your composition?\"\n\n\"For the composition. You're keeping the real report.\"\n\nIt was the first time I'd seen the two men comfortable with the fact that they could hear the same sound and be doing completely different jobs. Danse was afraid a beautiful explanation would get somebody hurt. Greg was afraid that treating every sound as a defect would make the world too quiet to bear.\n\nI remembered Rose and the songs Greg still chose not to play. The dead had never needed him to become incapable of making something new. I didn't say it aloud. That thought belonged to me until I knew whether he wanted to hear it.\n\nHe looked over his shoulder. \"You okay?\"\n\n\"Thinking.\"\n\n\"That's dangerous.\"\n\nI smiled. The edge of his jacket was damp with sea mist, and his hair had fallen across his forehead in a curl I wanted to push away. I waited until the recorder finished and the safety crew made their notes. Then I moved close enough to brush the curl aside.\n\n\"Thanks,\" he murmured.\n\nThe stage was still closed. The note had not become a secret message, a cure for grief, or permission to step beyond the barrier. It was a sound the workers could measure and a melody Greg might choose to carry home.\n\nGreg pulled out his notebook. On one page he wrote the sound as a musician would hear it; on the facing page he copied the time and gauge reading from the official report. The two pages weren't interchangeable.\n\n\"Why keep them separate?\" I asked.\n\n\"So nobody ends up reading my terrible chord notation as proof the pier is safe,\" he said.\n\nDanse looked at the notebook.\n\n\"That is a surprisingly good control measure.\"\n\n\"Hey. I'm full of those.\"\n\nGreg grinned, and I saw Danse's mouth almost move into a smile.\n\nThe wind made the stage sing again. Jo looked toward the boarded walkway, then returned to their post. The discipline of staying where the crew could actually help seemed as important as any discovery.\n\nFor once, I wasn't frightened by the sound simply because we didn't understand it. I was frightened in a useful way, the kind that made us ask who was responsible for the next decision.\n\nA gust tugged at Greg's coat. He caught my hand to steady himself, then let go when the path narrowed.\n\n\"See?\" he said. \"No heroic rescue required.\"\n\n\"Not unless you plan to rescue your notebook from the rain.\"\n\nHe tucked it under his shirt and ran toward the shelter, laughing.\n\nAt dusk, the water rose beneath the pier. The low note sounded several times, each one a little shorter than the last. Danse marked the timing, and Greg wrote down the pitch changes in ordinary musical language.\n\nThey compared the notes.\n\nIt wasn't a perfect match to the second bell at Tideglass. The structures were different. But both sounds appeared when water pressure changed along the old service system.\n\n\"That doesn't make them the same instrument,\" Greg said.\n\n\"No,\" Danse agreed. \"But it gives us a testable connection.\"\n\nWe sent the records to Moonroot Archive.\n\nSeverus and Neri could compare them with the damaged ledger and the older maintenance diagrams. Fen would continue monitoring the tide. Tamsin would not reopen the western pier until the supports passed the next inspection.\n\nNobody blamed a spirit.\n\nNobody pretended there wasn't danger.\n\nAfter the work, Greg sat beside me on the safe bench and looked toward the dark water.\n\n\"You know what's weird?\" he said.\n\n\"That the pier is singing?\"\n\n\"Besides that.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"I used to think music was something you made when you wanted people to hear you. Today the sound was telling us something we needed to listen to.\"\n\n\"That's what the fair was about.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Guess I finally learned my own lesson.\"\n\nI leaned against him.\n\nThe air smelled of rain. The first lanterns were coming on along the safe route, and the wind carried a faint note from the old wooden supports.\n\nGreg looked at me.\n\n\"Do you ever get scared that this place will stop being safe?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"But we know how to ask for help now. That's something.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe kissed my forehead.\n\nWe sat until the rain began, then walked back to the Starwake Door without crossing the closed section.\n\nIn the morning, the water had receded. The stage no longer made the note.\n\nTamsin recorded that too.\n\nGreg went home and wrote a short melody based on the sound, careful to keep the work separate from the inspection report.\n\nHe played it for me that evening.\n\nIt was strange and beautiful.\n\n\"It doesn't need words,\" I said.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"No. It knows what it wants to say.\"\n\nThe stage remained closed.\n\nThe music didn't have to be.","word_count":1617}}};for(const series of data.series){const byId=revisions[series.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of series.chapters){const r=byId[ch.id];if(!r||ch.chapter_number!==r.number)continue;ch.content=r.content;ch.word_count=r.word_count;ch.title=r.title;}}})();
